(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-clinical-reasoning-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Clinical Reasoning and Problem Solving — Quiz 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"\"If a patient is febrile and neutropenic, then use broad-spectrum antibiotics\" is given as an example of what?","choices":["The naturalistic, event-driven approach","A clinical guideline following an if-then rule","A quantitative technique using odds and probability","A counselling strategy for treatment adherence"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is a guideline built on an if-then rule. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Two clinicians order the same test with identical sensitivity and specificity, for two patients whose pretest probabilities differ substantially. What follows?","choices":["The test result means the same thing for both patients","The sensitivity and specificity of the test will differ between the two patients being evaluated","Neither patient's probability changes once the result is known","Their posttest probabilities will differ, because posttest probability builds on the pretest estimate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Posttest probability is the likelihood after the result is known, and it is the pretest likelihood revised by the test's sensitivity and specificity. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Specificity is related to fewer of which type of erroneous result, and is silent about which other?","choices":["Fewer false-negatives; silent about false-positives","Fewer false-positives; silent about false-negatives","Fewer true-positives; silent about true-negatives","Fewer false-positives; silent about true-positives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Specificity is related to fewer false-positives, and does not address false-negatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which body systems does the course description list as the focus of Clinical Medicine and Surgery I?","choices":["Neurology, psychiatry, endocrinology, and nephrology","Dermatology, ophthalmology, otorhinolaryngology, cardiology, pulmonology, and haematology-oncology","Musculoskeletal medicine, rheumatology, gastroenterology, urology, and reproductive endocrinology","Obstetrics, gynaecology, paediatrics, and geriatrics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These six are listed, with emphasis on etiology, clinical manifestations, diagnostic evaluation and management. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"When weighing a differential, which is the better bet?","choices":["Uncommon manifestations of common conditions","Common manifestations of uncommon conditions","Uncommon manifestations of uncommon conditions","Whichever diagnosis the pattern most quickly suggests"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Uncommon manifestations of common conditions beat common manifestations of uncommon ones. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the first step of the clinical reasoning process?","choices":["Gather initial patient information","Generate hypotheses","Plan the diagnostic and treatment strategy","Establish a working diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Step 1 is gathering initial patient information; interpretation, synthesis and hypothesis generation all follow it. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A test is described as able to correctly identify patients who do NOT have the disease. Which property is being described?","choices":["Pretest probability","Sensitivity","Specificity","Posttest probability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Specificity is the ability to correctly identify patients who do not have the disease or condition. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Besides the probability of disease, which two factors enter the decision to treat?","choices":["Sensitivity and specificity of the diagnostic test","Likelihood of success and the patient's ability to tolerate treatment","The base rate of the disease and the availability of a published clinical guideline","Health literacy and transportation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the to-treat-versus-not-to-treat heading. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Mental health challenges or cognitive impairment fall under which heading?","choices":["Elements of the FRAMES strategy","Steps of the clinical reasoning process","Barriers to treatment and medication adherence","Indications for the naturalistic approach"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. They are listed among the barriers, alongside cost, low health literacy, cultural or religious beliefs, fear of side effects, transportation and poor follow-up. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Being aware of the base rate of disease for the diagnoses on your differential list is recommended for what reason?","choices":["It removes the need to gather further data","It guarantees the working diagnosis will be correct","It is one of the suggestions for good decision-making","It replaces the need for clinical guidelines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. \"Be aware of base rate of disease for diagnoses on your differential list\" is listed among the suggestions for good decision-making, and base rate is how common a condition is. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Applying VINDICATE to a chief complaint of confusion, which pairing is correct?","choices":["Vascular — stroke, transient ischaemic attack, subarachnoid haemorrhage","Neoplastic — meningitis and encephalitis","Congenital — Alzheimer's disease and other dementias","Endocrine — traumatic brain injury and traumatic subdural haematoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the vascular causes of confusion. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which stage of the hypothetico-deductive method involves adding new diagnostic hypotheses to the differential list?","choices":["Hypothesis verification","Hypothesis refinement","Hypothesis generation","Patient management"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Refinement adds new diagnostic hypotheses triggered by data gathering to the differential list. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"For which specific behaviours do the Bates' Chapter 7 counselling guidelines apply?","choices":["Diet, exercise, and sleep hygiene","Medication timing, appointment keeping, and the sharing of records between providers","Weight control, hydration, and screen time","Unhealthy alcohol use, tobacco smoking cessation, and sexually transmitted infections"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Specific counselling guidelines for adults exist for these three. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which test property is best suited to screening, and which to confirming a diagnosis?","choices":["Specificity for screening; sensitivity for confirming","Sensitivity for both purposes","Sensitivity for screening; specificity for confirming","Neither property matters once pretest probability is known"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A highly sensitive test is best for screening, while a highly specific test is best for confirming a diagnosis. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three questions a clinician must answer?","choices":["What disease or underlying condition does this patient have?","Should testing be done?","Should this patient be treated?","Which clinician should take over the patient's care?"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three questions are what disease, should testing be done, and should this patient be treated. Transfer of care is not among them. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Among the suggestions for good decision-making, what should you do with your own hypothesis?","choices":["Ask questions designed to disprove it rather than confirm it","Ask questions designed to confirm it as quickly as possible","Avoid revisiting it once a working diagnosis is set","Accept it if it matches a familiar pattern"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ask questions to disprove rather than confirm your current hypothesis, and actively seek alternative diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a barrier to treatment and medication adherence?","choices":["Cost and affordability of medications or services","High health literacy","Strong communication and follow-up by the care team","Ready access to transportation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cost and affordability heads the list of barriers carried over from Patient-Centered Healthcare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which treatment principle applies?","choices":["If what you are doing is not working, increase the dose","If you do not know what to do, choose the most aggressive option","If what you are doing is working, change to something better","If what you are doing is not working, stop doing it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The three treatment principles are: if it is working keep doing it, if it is not working stop doing it, and if you do not know what to do, do not do anything. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Sensitivity is specifically related to fewer of which type of erroneous result?","choices":["Fewer false-positives","Fewer true-positives","Fewer true-negatives","Fewer false-negatives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity is related to fewer false-negatives, and does not address false-positives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Conditional probability refers to which of the following?","choices":["The proportion of a population that will develop a disease over a lifetime","The probability of disease if another event, test result, or condition is present","The probability that a treatment will be tolerated by the patient","The probability that two clinicians reach the same diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Conditional probability is the probability of disease or event given that another event, test result, or condition is present. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which element of FRAMES places the decision to change with the patient?","choices":["Responsibility of the patient","Feedback about personal risk","Empathetic style","Promote self-efficacy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES includes responsibility of the patient as a distinct element, alongside feedback, advice, a menu of options, an empathetic style and promoting self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Clinical decision making covers all of the following EXCEPT which?","choices":["Setting the sensitivity and specificity of the tests available","What information should be gathered from the patient at the outset","What diagnostics to order","What management a patient needs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sensitivity and specificity are fixed properties of a test, not decisions a clinician makes at the bedside. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does the hypothesis verification stage accomplish?","choices":["It generates the first list of possible diagnoses","It gathers data to monitor the patient's response to treatment","It selects which screening test to order first","It confirms the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the assembled data"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Verification is defined as confirming the fit of the diagnostic hypotheses with the data assembled. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What do benefit and risk encompass?","choices":["Only the medical consequences of treatment","Only the financial consequences of treatment","Neither, since benefit and risk are judged by the patient alone","Both financial and medical consequences"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Benefit and risk encompass both, and the balance must take into account the likelihood of disease and the magnitude of benefit and risk. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which aspects does the course say it will compare and contrast across conditions?","choices":["Only etiology and management","Only signs, symptoms, and diagnosis","Etiology, epidemiology, risk factors, clinical manifestations, diagnostic evaluation, management, patient education, referrals, and follow-up","Cost, local availability, expected turnaround time, and laboratory reporting format of every diagnostic test ordered for a given patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the full list given in the course description. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"The memory aid \"SpPin\" is intended to remind you of which relationship?","choices":["A highly sensitive test with a negative result rules the disease out","A highly specific test with a negative result rules the disease out","A highly specific test with a positive result rules the disease in","A highly sensitive test with a positive result rules the disease in"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. SpPin: a test with high Specificity and a Positive result helps rule in a disease — with specificity, a positive is a positive. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A highly sensitive test with a negative result is most useful for which purpose?","choices":["Confirming that the disease is present in that patient","Ruling the disease out in that patient","Establishing how common the disease is in the population","Determining whether the patient can tolerate treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. SnNout: a test with high Sensitivity and a Negative result helps rule out a disease. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which three cue sources feed the start of the hypothetico-deductive sequence?","choices":["Published guidelines, a literature review, and the cost data for each test","Risk, benefit, and likelihood of success","Initial history, initial examination, and screening diagnostic testing","Ask, advise, and assess"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The sequence opens with initial history cues, initial examination cues and screening diagnostic testing cues. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"The hypothetico-deductive method is best described as which of the following?","choices":["Treating the presenting signs and symptoms before a definitive diagnosis","Applying an if-then rule drawn from a published guideline","Recognising a familiar disease pattern and acting on it quickly","Proposing hypotheses and testing whether the data observed is consistent with them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It proposes hypotheses and tests whether they are acceptable by deciding if the data is consistent with what is observed. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Beyond treating before diagnosis, which two actions characterise the naturalistic approach?","choices":["Formulating a clinical question and appraising the literature","Ruling out the worst-case scenario and following responses to interventions","Calculating pretest probability and applying sensitivity and specificity","Assessing readiness to change and arranging follow-up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed under the naturalistic, event-driven approach. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
